{"seconds": 80.7, "exit": 0, "final": "Perfect! So the winning line is:\n1. **Rc1+** (check, forcing Ne1)\n2. **Rcxe1#** (checkmate)\n\nThis is a forced mate in 2! The motif is a **fork** or better said **deflection**\u2014the knight is forced to block the check and then gets mated when it captures it.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: Rc1+ forces Ne1 (the only legal move), and then Rcxe1 is checkmate because the rook gives check on e1 and the king has no escape squares (g2 pawn blocks, h1/h2/f1/f2 are controlled by the two rooks).\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Rc1+\", \"line\": [\"Rc1+\", \"Ne1\", \"Rcxe1#\"], \"motifs\": [\"deflection\", \"backRankMate\"]}\n```\n\n## Coaching Note\n\nYou had two rooks versus the knight. The winning move was **Rc1+**\u2014a forcing check that trapped White's king. White's only legal response was **Ne1**, blocking the check. Then **Rcxe1# is checkmate** because your rooks control all escape squares. The lesson: when your opponent's king is cramped, look for forcing checks that limit their options. A \"tempo move\" that forces a bad response often wins immediately.", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "8/1R1nkpp1/3Rp1p1/3pP3/3P4/5N2/P1r1r1PP/6K1 b - - 2 22"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "8/1R1nkpp1/3Rp1p1/3pP3/3P4/5N2/P1r1r1PP/6K1 b - - 2 22"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "8/1R1nkpp1/3Rp1p1/3pP3/3P4/5N2/P1r1r1PP/6K1 b - - 2 22"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "8/1R1nkpp1/3Rp1p1/3pP3/3P4/5N2/P1r1r1PP/6K1 b - - 2 22", "square": "e2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "8/1R1nkpp1/3Rp1p1/3pP3/3P4/5N2/P1r1r1PP/6K1 b - - 2 22", "square": "c2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-square_info", "args": {"fen": "8/1R1nkpp1/3Rp1p1/3pP3/3P4/5N2/P1r1r1PP/6K1 b - - 2 22", "square": "g2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-square_info", "args": {"fen": "8/1R1nkpp1/3Rp1p1/3pP3/3P4/5N2/P1r1r1PP/6K1 b - - 2 22", "square"